  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY NIGHT THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 143 PM MST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nThe precip should end Saturday night for most of our area, perhaps \npersisting in the Eastern Plains into the overnight, as the moisture \nand the 500mb jet pass to our southeast.\n\nThe cold front will bring much cooler conditions behind it, \nespecially for the eastern Plains, away from the mountains where\ntemperatures could dip 5-10 degF below normal during the day \nSunday. Wind will also die down near the mountains.\n\nMonday night into Tuesday presents our next best opportunity for \nrain, with a trough and a 700mb low in northeastern CO. POPs are \ngenerally below 50% along I-25, and above 50% both in the Plains and \nthe Mountains. Precip may begin for the mountains on Monday night, \nmoving into the I-25 corridor overnight, then beginning in the \nEastern Plains by sunrise. The window for precip to occur will be \nbrief, only a few hours in any one location. QPF total will be \nbetween 0.05 and 0.2 inches. The GFS still wants to make this more \nconsiderable of an event than the ECMWF, but ensemble members from \nboth models lean towards the ECMWF solution, so our official \nforecasts are very much on the lower side. The ECMWF has a shallower \nwave feature, while the GFS has a more robust low pressure. Any \nprecip that falls during night should fall as snow.\n\nAfter our Tuesday system, things look dry the rest of the week, with \na marginal chance of minor precip Thursday night into Friday. The \nupper-level forcing is there, but the flow and moisture looks too \nweak at this point.",
